{"target_index": 3, "field": "missing_row", "method": "obvious", "verdict": "blind_right", "corrected_value": {"authors": "Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K.", "year": "1978", "title": "Development of self-recognition in the infant.", "doi": "", "identified_by": "citation", "study_numbers": "", "replication_study_numbers": "1, 2", "type": "replication", "record_type": "replication", "outcome": "mixed"}, "quote": "The first experiment shows that only 2 out of 82 18- to 72-month-old Kenyan children
manifested self-oriented behaviors toward the mark, most of them freezing while staring at their
specular image. This result is in sharp contrast with the multiple studies reporting that by 20 months
of age, 60% to 85% of Western middle-class children pass the mark test (Amsterdam, 1972;
Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979). The question is how to explain this", "location": "Parent-selected exact primary passage, character offsets33353:33818; Discussion", "reason": "Bertenthal and Fisher (1978) is attributed not only in the introduction's 18-to-24-month Western benchmark set that the paper attempts to replicate across cultures, but also in the Method and General Discussion sections as a direct source of the >60% (60% to 85%) pass-rate benchmark tested via the binomial analysis and contrasted with the cross-cultural results. Rejecting it in favor of Amsterdam (1972) confuses Amsterdam's role as the original paradigm source with the empirical benchmark findings directly re-tested here. Parent independently read the actual primary Results/Discussion contrast and original-specific references. This new exact quotation replaces older whitespace-normalized evidence; raw model replies and prior decision remain preserved. This identity recheck preserves the previously independently verified outcome; no new outcome is inferred from the quoted identity passage."}

